---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { guides, guidePath } from '../../data/guides';

const guide = guides.find((g) => g.slug === 'clean-up-claude-code-worktrees')!;
const other = guidePath('cannot-delete-branch-used-by-worktree');
const docs = 'https://code.claude.com/docs/en/worktrees';
const toc = [
  { id: 'short-answer', label: 'The short answer' },
  { id: 'where-they-live', label: 'Where they live' },
  { id: 'what-claude-cleans', label: 'What Claude cleans up' },
  { id: 'find-the-stale-ones', label: 'Find the stale ones' },
  { id: 'check-first', label: 'Check before you remove' },
  { id: 'remove-them', label: 'Remove them' },
  { id: 'leftover-folder', label: 'The leftover folder error' },
  { id: 'stop-the-pile', label: 'Stop the pile growing' },
  { id: 'many-worktrees', label: 'When there are dozens' },
];
---
<Guide guide={guide} toc={toc}>
  <Fragment slot="checked">
    Checked against the <a class="link" href={docs} rel="noopener">Claude Code docs</a> on 17 October 2026. Claude Code changes quickly, so
    compare with your own install. The git commands and output were run on git 2.54.0 (Apple Git-157) in a throwaway
    repository with the same layout Claude Code uses, created by hand with <code>git worktree add</code>. Paths are shortened to <code>~/code</code>.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
  <p>
    Claude Code removes some of its worktrees itself and leaves others, on purpose. To clean up the rest, list them with <code>git worktree list</code>, check each one for work that exists only there, remove the clean ones with <code>git worktree remove</code>, delete the leftover branches with <code>git branch -d</code>, and run <code>git worktree prune</code> for folders that are already gone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where-they-live">Where they live</h2>
  <p>
    With <code>claude --worktree &lt;name&gt;</code> (or <code>-w</code>), Claude Code creates the worktree at <code>.claude/worktrees/&lt;name&gt;/</code> in your repository, on a new branch called <code>worktree-&lt;name&gt;</code> (<a class="link" href={docs} rel="noopener">docs</a>). If you leave the name out, it picks one such as <code>bright-running-fox</code>. The desktop app uses the same folder by default and lets you change it in its settings.
    Some older reports describe a different folder, <code>~/.claude-worktrees/&lt;project&gt;/</code> (<a class="link" href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/26725" rel="noopener">issue 26725</a>), so if <code>.claude/worktrees</code> is empty on your machine, look there too.
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git worktree list
~/code/web                                      cb3c050 [main]
~/code/web/.claude/worktrees/bright-running-fox cb3c050 [worktree-bright-running-fox]
~/code/web/.claude/worktrees/checkout-redesign  8efbfad [worktree-checkout-redesign]
~/code/web/.claude/worktrees/headless-run       cb3c050 [worktree-headless-run] locked
~/code/web/.claude/worktrees/old-spike          102cd2c [worktree-old-spike]</code></pre>
  <p>
    The first line is your main checkout. A folder under <code>.claude/worktrees</code> and a branch starting with <code>worktree-</code> are the quickest way to recognise Claude Code's work. The <code>locked</code> marker is
    explained below.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-claude-cleans">What Claude Code cleans up, and what it keeps</h2>
  <p>This is what the <a class="link" href={docs} rel="noopener">official docs</a> say about removal:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A session you end with <code>/exit</code> and that changed nothing,</strong> started without a name: the worktree and its branch are removed automatically.</li>
    <li><strong>A named session, or one with changes or new commits:</strong> Claude asks whether to keep or remove it. Keeping leaves the folder and the branch in place.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-interactive runs (<code>claude -p</code>):</strong> there is no exit prompt, so these worktrees are not cleaned up and can stay locked.</li>
    <li><strong>Subagent worktrees</strong> (<code>isolation: worktree</code>): removed when the subagent finishes without changes. Ones with changes stay until a periodic sweep removes those older than <code>cleanupPeriodDays</code>, 30 days by default, and that sweep leaves anything with changed, untracked or unpushed work.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Two more cases come from user reports, not the docs. A crash or a closed terminal never reaches the exit step, which
    is how stale worktrees pile up (<a class="link" href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/26725" rel="noopener">issue 26725</a>).
    And the VS Code extension was reported to skip cleanup when you close the tab
    (<a class="link" href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/31488" rel="noopener">issue 31488</a>, now closed, so
    check your version).
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything that holds work is kept deliberately, and that is the right call. It is also why the folder fills up.
  </p>

  <h2 id="find-the-stale-ones">Find the stale ones</h2>
  <p>
    Sort the branches by their last commit to see which worktrees have been idle the longest:
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git for-each-ref --sort=committerdate 'refs/heads/worktree-*' --format='%(committerdate:short)  %(refname:short)'
2026-08-01  worktree-bright-running-fox
2026-08-01  worktree-headless-run
2026-08-20  worktree-old-spike
2026-10-15  worktree-checkout-redesign</code></pre>
  <p>And see how much space each one takes:</p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> du -sh .claude/worktrees/*
 12K .claude/worktrees/bright-running-fox
 12K .claude/worktrees/checkout-redesign
8.0K .claude/worktrees/headless-run
 12K .claude/worktrees/old-spike</code></pre>
  <p class="term-note">
    These are tiny because the test repository is tiny. Each worktree is a full copy of your files, and one with
    dependencies installed is far larger.
  </p>
  <p>
    Claude Code locks a worktree while an agent is running in it. A <code>locked</code> marker in the list means a run is
    active, or ended without releasing the lock. A <code>prunable</code> marker means the folder is gone. Both matter
    below.
  </p>

  <h2 id="check-first">Check before you remove anything</h2>
  <p>
    The exit prompt exists because a worktree can hold the only copy of someone's work. Check the same two things Claude
    does. First, commits that are not in your main branch:
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git log --oneline main..worktree-checkout-redesign
8efbfad redesign checkout</code></pre>
  <p>Second, files that were never committed:</p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git -C .claude/worktrees/bright-running-fox status --short
?? notes.txt</code></pre>
  <p>
    Empty output means clean. Anything listed lives only in that folder. Git refuses to remove such a worktree, which is a
    stop sign and not an obstacle:
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git worktree remove .claude/worktrees/bright-running-fox
<span class="e">fatal: '.claude/worktrees/bright-running-fox' contains modified or untracked files, use --force to delete it</span></code></pre>
  <p>
    The <a class="link" href={other}>first guide</a> goes through these checks in more detail, including what to do when
    the branch is not merged.
  </p>

  <h2 id="remove-them">Remove them</h2>
  <p>
    For a clean worktree that is not locked, <code>git worktree remove</code> followed by <code>git branch -d</code> is all
    it takes. A locked one needs one extra step, because git will not remove it:
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git worktree remove .claude/worktrees/headless-run
<span class="e">fatal: cannot remove a locked working tree, lock reason: claude agent running (pid 4242)</span>
<span class="d">use 'remove -f -f' to override or unlock first</span></code></pre>
  <p>
    Make sure no agent is really still running there, because that is what the lock says. If it is stale, unlock, remove,
    and delete the branch:
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git worktree unlock .claude/worktrees/headless-run
<span class="p">$</span> git worktree remove .claude/worktrees/headless-run
<span class="p">$</span> git branch -d worktree-headless-run
Deleted branch worktree-headless-run (was cb3c050).</code></pre>
  <p>
    If the folder is already gone, the worktree shows as <code>prunable</code> and <code>git worktree prune</code> forgets
    it:
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git worktree list
~/code/web                                      cb3c050 [main]
~/code/web/.claude/worktrees/old-spike          102cd2c [worktree-old-spike] prunable

<span class="p">$</span> git worktree prune -v
Removing worktrees/old-spike: gitdir file points to non-existent location</code></pre>
  <p>
    Prune only forgets worktrees whose folders are missing. It does not delete the branch, and a worktree on an external
    drive that is not plugged in also looks missing, so plug the drive in first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="leftover-folder">The "already exists" error when you start again</h2>
  <p>
    A cleanup that removed git's record but not the folder leaves a directory behind. The next <code>claude -w</code> with
    the same name then fails, because git will not create a worktree in a folder that already has files in it. This was <a class="link" href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/45737" rel="noopener">reported</a> and the issue is
    closed, but the same state can come from a crash. You see git's own message:
  </p>
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git worktree add .claude/worktrees/benchmark_only -b worktree-benchmark_only
Preparing worktree (new branch 'worktree-benchmark_only')
<span class="e">fatal: '.claude/worktrees/benchmark_only' already exists</span></code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>git worktree list</code> does not show it, because git no longer knows about it. Look inside the folder before
    you delete it, since it may hold files you want, and then remove the leftover folder by hand.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stop-the-pile">Stop the pile growing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Ignore the folder.</strong> The docs suggest adding <code>.claude/worktrees/</code> to your <code>.gitignore</code>. Without it, every worktree shows up as untracked files in your main checkout:
<pre class="term"><code><span class="p">$</span> git status --short
?? .claude/

<span class="p">$</span> echo ".claude/worktrees/" &gt;&gt; .gitignore
<span class="p">$</span> git status --short
?? .gitignore</code></pre>
    </li>
    <li><strong>End terminal sessions with <code>/exit</code>.</strong> That is when Claude runs its keep-or-remove check.</li>
    <li><strong>In the desktop app,</strong> archive a session when you are done with it, so its worktree goes with it.</li>
    <li><strong>Look now and then.</strong> <code>git worktree list</code> takes a second and shows what git itself knows, whatever any tool believes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="many-worktrees">When there are dozens</h2>
  <p>
    A few worktrees are easy to handle by hand. After weeks of parallel agents there can be dozens across several
    repositories, and the checks above have to be run for each one. That is what Lopper automates, described below.
    It recognises Claude Code's worktrees by their folder and branch names, along with those from Cursor, Codex and
    Conductor, and shows for each one whether it is safe to remove.
  </p>
</Guide>
